<%# Mortgage result (spec issue #187, DESIGN.md §4) — a tabular-output + charts result built
    on the same components as Amortization, extended for the multi-component monthly payment.
    It composes several BLEND components:
      • Hero-result   — the TOTAL monthly payment (5px coral left rule, big tabular-nums),
                        with the P&I portion called out beneath it.
      • Stat grid     — loan amount, total of P&I, total interest, payments (the shared
                        responsive `.stat-grid`, `--wide` because the totals run high-magnitude
                        — a $1M home over 30y clears seven figures of total cost).
      • Charts        — a Chart.js donut of the FOUR monthly-payment components (largest slice
                        P&I in green per DESIGN.md §1, then coral/amber/quiet, hover tooltips)
                        + a TradingView lightweight-charts balance-over-time line/area
                        (crosshair + per-point tooltip). Both keep an always-rendered NO-JS
                        fallback (a text legend / SVG curve) so the breakdown never gates on JS
                        and never relies on colour alone (DESIGN.md §4 / §6); the Stimulus
                        controller hides the fallback only once it has drawn the live chart.
      • Data table    — the month-by-month P&I schedule (thin rule borders, right-aligned
                        tabular-nums, a hard ink total row), paginated by Stimulus with a
                        no-JS full-table baseline.
    The backend owns every number (ARCHITECTURE.md §4); this only renders the envelope —
    money strings, the chart series, and the schedule rows. `calc:` is a valid
    Calculators::Mortgage. %>
<% r = calc.result %>
<% chart = r[:chart] %>
<% slices = mortgage_donut(chart) %>
<% schedule = r[:schedule] %>

<div class="space-y-6"
     data-controller="mortgage"
     data-mortgage-donut-value="<%= mortgage_donut_data(slices).to_json %>"
     data-mortgage-curve-value="<%= chart[:balance_curve].to_json %>">
  <%# ── Hero: the total monthly payment ──────────────────────────────────────
      The hero is the TOTAL monthly payment (P&I + tax + insurance + HOA) — the headline
      answer a homebuyer wants. The P&I portion is called out beneath it so the loan part of
      the payment stays legible. %>
  <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-[18px] border border-border bg-surface shadow-[0_10px_34px_rgba(150,110,80,0.12)]">
    <div class="flex gap-5 p-7">
      <span class="w-[5px] shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <div class="min-w-0">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-accent">Monthly payment</p>
        <div class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 gap-y-1">
          <p class="text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-[-0.04em] tabular-nums text-ink">$<%= money_display(r[:total_monthly]) %></p>
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-faint">/ month</p>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Including <span class="font-semibold text-ink">$<%= money_display(r[:pi_monthly]) %></span> principal &amp; interest,
          over <%= pluralize(r[:number_of_payments], "monthly payment") %>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# ── Stat grid: the loan totals (DESIGN.md §4 "Stat grid", issue #143) ──────
      The shared responsive stat grid. `--wide` (13rem track) because these are HIGH-MAGNITUDE
      money figures — a $1M home over 30y pushes the total of P&I and the total interest into
      seven figures, which would wrap mid-number on the default 9.5rem track (DESIGN.md §4 —
      single-line preferred for big figures; the `.stat-card` overflow-wrap net is the floor).
      This grid sits directly on the page background (a top-level block, not nested in the
      hero card), so the cards use `.stat-card--surface` to stand off the cream. Total interest
      reads in coral (the cost figure). %>
  <dl class="stat-grid stat-grid--wide">
    <div class="stat-card stat-card--surface">
      <dt class="text-xs font-bold text-label">Loan amount</dt>
      <dd class="mt-1.5 break-words text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-[-0.02em] tabular-nums text-ink">$<%= money_display(r[:loan_amount]) %></dd>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card stat-card--surface">
      <dt class="text-xs font-bold text-label">Total of P&amp;I payments</dt>
      <dd class="mt-1.5 break-words text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-[-0.02em] tabular-nums text-ink">$<%= money_display(r[:total_of_pi]) %></dd>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card stat-card--surface">
      <dt class="text-xs font-bold text-label">Total interest</dt>
      <dd class="mt-1.5 break-words text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-[-0.02em] tabular-nums text-primary">$<%= money_display(r[:total_interest]) %></dd>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card stat-card--surface">
      <dt class="text-xs font-bold text-label">Number of payments</dt>
      <dd class="mt-1.5 break-words text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-[-0.02em] tabular-nums text-ink"><%= number_with_delimiter(r[:number_of_payments]) %></dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <%# ── Charts: payment-component donut + balance curve (DESIGN.md §4 "Charts") ─
      Each chart is a [JS canvas (hidden until drawn)] + [static no-JS fallback (shown)].
      The Stimulus controller draws the live, hover-capable chart into the canvas and only
      then hides the fallback (retire()). With no JS the fallback stays — the breakdown is
      reachable without JS and never colour-alone (DESIGN.md §4 / §6). %>
  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%# Donut — the four monthly-payment components. Chart.js draws the hover-tooltip
        doughnut; the figures repeat as a text legend below (always shown) so the breakdown
        reads without colour and without JS. %>
    <div class="rounded-[18px] border border-border bg-surface p-6 shadow-[0_10px_34px_rgba(150,110,80,0.12)]">
      <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-faint">Monthly payment breakdown</p>
      <div class="mt-4 flex items-center gap-5">
        <%# Live Chart.js donut canvas — hidden until the controller draws it. The canvas
            carries explicit pixel dimensions so Chart.js (responsive:false) sizes from the
            element itself, not from a parent measured before layout reflows — which on a
            slower (CI) render left the canvas 0×0 and blank. 112px = the size-28 box. %>
        <div class="relative size-28 shrink-0" data-mortgage-target="donutCanvas" hidden>
          <canvas width="112" height="112" class="size-28"></canvas>
        </div>

        <%# No-JS fallback donut — a CSS conic-gradient composed from the slice token colours
            (no inline hex; DESIGN.md §5 guardrail), largest slice green (DESIGN.md §1). %>
        <div class="relative size-28 shrink-0 rounded-full" role="img"
             data-mortgage-target="donutFallback"
             aria-label="Monthly payment breakdown: <%= slices.map { |s| "#{s[:label]} $#{money_display(s[:amount])}" }.join(', ') %>"
             style="background: <%= mortgage_donut_gradient(slices) %>;">
          <span class="absolute inset-[22%] rounded-full bg-surface" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </div>

        <%# Text legend (always shown) — the breakdown without colour (DESIGN.md §6). %>
        <dl class="min-w-0 flex-1 space-y-2.5">
          <% slices.each do |slice| %>
            <div>
              <dt class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-bold text-label">
                <span class="inline-block size-2.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-<%= slice[:color] %>" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                <%= slice[:label] %>
              </dt>
              <dd class="mt-0.5 pl-4.5 text-base font-extrabold tabular-nums text-ink">$<%= money_display(slice[:amount]) %></dd>
            </div>
          <% end %>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>

    <%# Balance over time — lightweight-charts draws the crosshair+tooltip area/line into
        the canvas; the no-JS fallback is an SVG area+line from the same backend samples,
        with the endpoints labelled so the trend reads without colour or JS. %>
    <div class="rounded-[18px] border border-border bg-surface p-6 shadow-[0_10px_34px_rgba(150,110,80,0.12)]">
      <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-faint">Balance over time</p>

      <%# Live lightweight-charts canvas — hidden until the controller draws it. %>
      <div class="mt-4 h-32 w-full" data-mortgage-target="curveCanvas" hidden></div>

      <%# No-JS fallback — the SVG curve + endpoint labels (DESIGN.md §6: not colour-alone). %>
      <div data-mortgage-target="curveFallback">
        <% points = mortgage_curve_points(chart[:balance_curve]) %>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" preserveAspectRatio="none" class="mt-4 h-28 w-full" role="img"
             aria-label="Remaining balance falling from $<%= money_display(chart[:balance_curve].first[:balance]) %> to $0.00 over the term">
          <polygon points="0,100 <%= points %> 100,100" fill="var(--color-accent)" fill-opacity="0.12" />
          <polyline points="<%= points %>" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-accent)" stroke-width="2"
                    vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
        </svg>
        <div class="mt-3 flex items-baseline justify-between text-xs font-semibold text-faint">
          <span class="tabular-nums">$<%= money_display(chart[:balance_curve].first[:balance]) %></span>
          <span class="tabular-nums">$0.00</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# ── Schedule (DESIGN.md §4 "Data table") ────────────────────────────────
      The full month-by-month P&I schedule (tax/insurance/HOA do not amortize the loan).
      Long (a 30y mortgage is 360 rows), so Stimulus paginates: the first PAGE rows show, the
      rest are revealed by "Show all". The no-JS baseline keeps every row present and the
      wrapper scrolls (max-height + overflow), so the table is fully usable without JS. %>
  <% page = 12 %>
  <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-[18px] border border-border bg-surface shadow-[0_10px_34px_rgba(150,110,80,0.12)]">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-2 px-6 pt-6">
      <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-faint">Amortization schedule</p>
      <p class="text-xs text-muted tabular-nums"><%= pluralize(schedule.length, "month") %></p>
    </div>
    <%# data-mortgage-target=scroll: no-JS scroll cap; the controller removes it when it takes
        over pagination so revealed rows aren't trapped in a scroll box. %>
    <div class="mt-4 max-h-[28rem] overflow-y-auto" data-mortgage-target="scroll">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="sticky top-0 z-10 bg-surface">
          <tr class="border-b border-rule text-left text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.08em] text-faint">
            <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-2.5 font-bold">Month</th>
            <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-2.5 text-right font-bold">Payment</th>
            <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-2.5 text-right font-bold">Principal</th>
            <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-2.5 text-right font-bold">Interest</th>
            <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-2.5 text-right font-bold">Balance</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <% schedule.each_with_index do |row, i| %>
            <%# Rows past the first page start hidden ONLY when JS is active — the controller
                adds the cap on connect and toggles it. data-index drives the "Show N more"
                math. Marked with the row class so the controller can find them. %>
            <tr class="border-b border-rule/70 text-ink last:border-0 mortgage-row"
                data-mortgage-target="row" data-index="<%= i %>">
              <td class="px-6 py-2.5 tabular-nums text-muted"><%= row[:month] %></td>
              <td class="px-6 py-2.5 text-right tabular-nums">$<%= money_display(row[:payment]) %></td>
              <td class="px-6 py-2.5 text-right tabular-nums">$<%= money_display(row[:principal]) %></td>
              <td class="px-6 py-2.5 text-right tabular-nums text-primary">$<%= money_display(row[:interest]) %></td>
              <td class="px-6 py-2.5 text-right tabular-nums font-semibold"><%= row[:balance] == "0.00" ? "Paid off" : "$#{money_display(row[:balance])}" %></td>
            </tr>
          <% end %>
        </tbody>
        <tfoot>
          <%# Hard total row — top border in ink (DESIGN.md §4 "Data table"). The schedule is
              the P&I schedule, so its total is total_of_pi (loan amount + total interest). %>
          <tr class="border-t-2 border-ink bg-bg/40 font-bold text-ink">
            <td class="px-6 py-3">Total</td>
            <td class="px-6 py-3 text-right tabular-nums">$<%= money_display(r[:total_of_pi]) %></td>
            <td class="px-6 py-3 text-right tabular-nums">$<%= money_display(r[:loan_amount]) %></td>
            <td class="px-6 py-3 text-right tabular-nums text-primary">$<%= money_display(r[:total_interest]) %></td>
            <td class="px-6 py-3"></td>
          </tr>
        </tfoot>
      </table>
    </div>

    <%# Show-all control — hidden unless JS reveals it AND there's more than one page (the
        controller only shows it when schedule.length > page). It expands the hidden rows. %>
    <div class="hidden border-t border-rule px-6 py-4 text-center" data-mortgage-target="toggle">
      <button type="button" data-action="mortgage#showAll"
              class="cursor-pointer text-sm font-semibold text-primary transition-opacity hover:opacity-80 focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-accent">
        Show all <span class="tabular-nums"><%= schedule.length %></span> months
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# Page-size hint for the Stimulus controller (the first page count). %>
  <span class="hidden" data-mortgage-target="pageSize" data-page="<%= page %>"></span>
</div>
